<!-- ═══════════════ SECTION 3: PRICE SPREAD ═══════════════ -->
<section id="price-spread-analysis">
  <h2>3. Price Spread &amp; Market Competitiveness</h2>

  <p>Price spread — the gap between the cheapest and most expensive stations for the same fuel — is a direct measure of market competitiveness. Wide spreads suggest that consumers who do not shop around may pay significantly more than necessary. The Interquartile Range (IQR) captures the band within which the middle 50% of prices fall, representing what most consumers actually encounter.</p>

  <div class="chart">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart4_b64}" alt="Price spread range chart by fuel type">
    <p style="font-size:0.85em;color:var(--neutral);margin-top:4px;">Figure 4: Visual representation of full price range (line), IQR (shaded box), and median (diamond) for each fuel type.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="chart">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart5_b64}" alt="Price spread and IQR bar comparison">
    <p style="font-size:0.85em;color:var(--neutral);margin-top:4px;">Figure 5: Side-by-side comparison of total price spread (Max−Min) and Interquartile Range (Q3−Q1) per fuel type.</p>
  </div>

