<!-- ═══════════════ SECTION 6: KEY FINDINGS ═══════════════ -->
<section id="key-findings">
  <h2>6. Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>

  <p>The following synthesised findings are drawn directly from the data analysis above and are intended to inform evidence-based policy decisions around fuel market regulation and consumer protection.</p>

  <div class="finding">
    <span class="num">1</span>
    <strong>Fuel prices vary significantly by grade, with a clear hierarchy:</strong>
    Premium fuels ({most_expensive['fuel_type']} at {fmt_p(most_expensive['mean_price'])}/L average) command substantial premiums over standard grades ({least_expensive['fuel_type']} at {fmt_p(least_expensive['mean_price'])}/L average). The price differential between the cheapest and most expensive fuel types is <strong>{fmt_p(most_expensive['mean_price'] - least_expensive['mean_price'])}/L</strong>.
    <br><em>Policy implication:</em> The premium fuel market shows less price sensitivity. Transparency tools should help consumers understand whether premium fuel price premiums are justified by actual performance benefits.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <span class="num">2</span>
