<section id="key-findings">
  <h2>6. Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>

  <p>The following synthesised findings are drawn directly from the data analysis above and are intended to inform evidence-based policy decisions around fuel market regulation and consumer protection.</p>

  <div class="finding">
    <span class="num">1</span>
    <strong>Fuel prices vary significantly by grade, with a clear hierarchy:</strong>
    Premium fuels ({most_expensive['fuel_type']} at {fmt_p(most_expensive['mean_price'])}/L average) command substantial premiums over standard grades ({least_expensive['fuel_type']} at {fmt_p(least_expensive['mean_price'])}/L average). The price differential between the cheapest and most expensive fuel types is <strong>{fmt_p(most_expensive['mean_price'] - least_expensive['mean_price'])}/L</strong>.
    <br><em>Policy implication:</em> The premium fuel market shows less price sensitivity. Transparency tools should help consumers understand whether premium fuel price premiums are justified by actual performance benefits.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <span class="num">2</span>
    <strong>Considerable price spreads exist within each fuel type:</strong>
    For {highest_spread['fuel_type']}, the gap between the cheapest and most expensive station is <strong>{highest_spread['price_spread']:.1f}p/L</strong> — equivalent to <strong>£{(highest_spread['price_spread'] * 55 / 100):.2f}</strong> on a 55-litre tank fill. Even within the IQR, consumers face price differences of {highest_iqr['iqr']:.1f}p/L for {highest_iqr['fuel_type']}.
    <br><em>Policy implication:</em> Active price comparison can yield meaningful consumer savings. The Fuel Finder scheme should be promoted more widely, and consideration given to mandating clear on-forecourt price displays visible from the roadside.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <span class="num">3</span>
    <strong>Update lag data reveals inconsistent retailer reporting compliance:</strong>
    With a median update lag of <strong>{overall_median_lag:.1f} hours</strong> but a 90th percentile of <strong>{overall_p90_lag:.1f} hours</strong> ({overall_p90_lag/24:.1f} days), a significant tail of stations are not maintaining current price data. <strong>{freshness_stats.loc[freshness_stats['stale_pct'].idxmax(), 'fuel_type']}</strong> shows the highest staleness rate with {freshness_stats.loc[freshness_stats['stale_pct'].idxmax(), 'stale_pct']:.1f}% of records over a week old.
    <br><em>Policy implication:</em> Consider introducing a maximum update interval (e.g., 24–48 hours) as a condition of the open data mandate, with enforcement mechanisms for persistent non-compliance.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <span class="num">4</span>
    <strong>Station coverage varies materially by fuel type:</strong>
    {most_covered} is near-universal ({fuel_type_stations[most_covered]/total_stations_for_pct*100:.1f}% station coverage), while {least_covered} is available at only {fuel_type_stations[least_covered]/total_stations_for_pct*100:.1f}% of stations. This limits price comparison utility for consumers purchasing less common fuel types.
    <br><em>Policy implication:</em> When assessing market competitiveness for niche fuel types, account for the reduced number of competing stations. Competition metrics should be calculated per fuel type, not aggregated.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <span class="num">5</span>
    <strong>The data infrastructure is functional but could be strengthened:</strong>
    With <strong>{total_records:,} records</strong> from <strong>{unique_stations:,} stations</strong> and zero missing values, the data collection system is operating at scale. However, the lack of station metadata (brand, geography, ownership) severely limits the depth of analysis possible for antitrust and regional disparity assessment.
    <br><em>Policy implication:</em> Enriching the open data scheme with station location (at minimum postcode sector) and brand affiliation would dramatically increase its utility for competition monitoring without compromising commercial sensitivity.
